WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.130
How does Netflix's "Vikings: Valhalla" relate
to the original History series?

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.490
Is the same creative team making the sequel?

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:07.959
Will it be historically accurate?

00:00:07.959 --> 00:00:11.240
Keep watching for all those answers and a
lot more.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.330
Just when you thought you had a handle on
all the various descendants, relatives and

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:18.259
acquaintances of Ragnar Lothbrok, the story
of "Valhalla" jumps more than 100 years into

00:00:18.259 --> 00:00:19.259
the future.

00:00:19.259 --> 00:00:23.560
According to a Netflix press packet, the sequel
series begins in the year 1002, kicking off

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.240
with a "particular historical event."

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.740
That could be the Viking attack on London
Bridge led by Olaf Haraldsson in the trailer,

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:33.400
but historically, that event didn't take place
until several years later, if it happened

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.910
at all, so it's more likely something else.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:40.930
Another candidate is the St. Brice's Day Massacre,
in which King Aethelred II ordered the slaughter

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.680
of all the Danish Vikings living in English
territory, hoping to put an end to years of

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:45.690
Viking raids.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:49.940
Instead, King Aethelred's actions provoked
the Danish king Sweyn Forkbeard to increase

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.210
the frequency and severity of his attacks,
eventually forcing Aethelred and his queen,

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:55.920
Emma of Normandy, into exile.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.640
Considering many of the main players in the
St. Brice's Day Massacre are confirmed characters

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.150
for the first season of "Vikings: Valhalla,"
it seems like a strong, dramatic contender

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.961
to kick off the series.

00:01:04.961 --> 00:01:06.100
"The entire North is threatened."

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.030
"By the English king."

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:11.490
Whatever event marks the beginning of "Vikings:
Valhalla," prepare to see a significantly

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.229
more complex political landscape than in "Vikings."

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:18.189
In the Netflix press packet, showrunner Jeb
Stuart explains what the world will look like:

00:01:18.189 --> 00:01:21.499
"The Vikings had been living in the British
Isles and had settled into big areas called

00:01:21.499 --> 00:01:22.499
the Danelaw.

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:26.860
The Danelaw had started to grow — not just
with Danes, but also with Swedes, and Norwegians,

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.149
and folks from all over Scandinavia.

00:01:29.149 --> 00:01:32.399
[...] Saxon nobles were afraid that, if you
went out another generation, suddenly there

00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:35.670
wouldn't be any difference between Saxons
and Vikings."

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:39.270
The Vikings on "Vikings" spent most of their
time engaged in power struggles, but there

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.539
was one man in control behind the camera.

00:01:41.539 --> 00:01:45.549
Creator Michael Hirst wrote every single episode
of "Vikings," so perhaps it's not surprising

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:47.590
that he'll be less involved in the sequel
series.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:51.479
Hirst will still be an executive producer,
alongside "Vikings" vets Morgan O'Sullivan

00:01:51.479 --> 00:01:53.080
and showrunner Jeb Stuart.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:56.000
Stuart you may know as one of the co-writers
of "Die Hard."

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.840
"Welcome to the party, pal."

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.880
Given the time jump, we likely won't be seeing
any of the familiar faces from "Vikings,"

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:04.529
unless they appear as ghosts, visions, or
in flashbacks.

00:02:04.529 --> 00:02:08.860
Fortunately, the same person who worked his
casting magic for "Vikings" is back for "Valhalla."

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:12.569
Frank Moiselle returns as casting director,
and has filled the new series with cast members

00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:16.110
O'Sullivan says "bring a fresh, youthful energy
to this story."

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.660
Also back is "Vikings" historical consultant
Justin Pollard.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.550
He will be the one digging into the obscure
history being explored by the show, working

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.400
to make sure it's as accurate as possible.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.580
He spent months consulting with Stuart on
the new series before writing even started.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.790
As for the shooting location, "Vikings: Valhalla"
is being shot in Ireland, just like the original

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:37.019
series, so there will be the same breathtaking
scenery fans expect to see.

00:02:37.019 --> 00:02:40.430
"Vikings" had one indisputable lead character:
Ragnar Lothbrok.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.579
"Vikings: Valhalla," though, will have three
central characters sharing the spotlight:

00:02:44.579 --> 00:02:48.970
legendary Viking explorer Leif Eriksson, pagan
shieldmaiden Freydis Eriksdotter, and the

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:52.230
Norwegian prince and Viking berserker Harald
Sigurdsson.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:55.620
Brother and sister Leif and Freydis will meet
Harald for the first time during the events

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:56.620
of the series.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.209
Stuart says he chose to build the story around
these three characters because of what they

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:01.209
represent.

00:03:01.209 --> 00:03:02.250
In the Netflix press kit, he says:

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:05.450
"For me, they each embody different elements
of the Viking spirit.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:09.382
Bringing Leif and Freydís — who are both
Greenlanders — to Norway, to Kattegat, and

00:03:09.382 --> 00:03:13.019
embedding them in the Viking story just seemed
a very natural place to go."

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:16.450
Centering "Vikings: Valhalla" around three
characters will also help offer a broader

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:20.099
perspective on the culture and characters,
which will be important to help modern viewers

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:21.380
relate to the story.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:22.380
Stuart added:

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.380
"It's a timeless story.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:27.160
In a strange way — if you take massive violence
out of the equation — the Vikings embody

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.859
some of the elements that we still most want
for ourselves.

00:03:29.859 --> 00:03:33.340
[...] Our characters are dealing with relationships
and issues that are just as relevant today

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.280
as they were a thousand years ago, and those
stories will always excite and captivate us."

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.709
While some suspected that the title "Vikings:
Valhalla" meant a glimpse into the Viking

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:45.390
afterlife, we now know that the title of the
series isn't necessarily about that sort of

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:46.540
life after death.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:50.189
The title "Valhalla" was chosen because the
show depicts the events that lead to the end

00:03:50.189 --> 00:03:53.650
of the Viking Age, which historically ended
in 1066.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:54.650
Stuart said:

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.739
"We played with a lot of new titles and kept
coming back to 'Valhalla' because the idea

00:03:57.739 --> 00:04:00.249
of the show is inherently tied to the end
of this era.

00:04:00.249 --> 00:04:03.580
It's all about showing this incredible time
in history — and how all things eventually

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:04.580
come to an end."

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.680
But don't get too worried just yet about the
end of the "Vikings" era.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.900
Since the series begins in 1002, the show
has more than six decades to play around with

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.370
before the Viking Age comes to a historical
close.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:18.510
And it's not like any period of history ever
came to a neat stop, so we wouldn't worry

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.800
about running out of Viking legends to dramatize
any time soon.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.289
Frida Gustavsson will be playing Freydis Eriksdotter,
the daughter of Erik the Red and sister of

00:04:27.289 --> 00:04:28.750
Leif Eriksson, on "Valhalla."

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:33.139
In an uncanny coincidence, Gustavsson's real-life
father is also named Erik, and her real-life

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:34.520
brother is also named Leif.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.069
And it doesn't end there.

00:04:36.069 --> 00:04:37.990
Gustavsson's real-life nickname is also "Freydis."

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:42.050
No wonder she really threw herself into her
role, reading multiple books to prepare, with

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:45.199
her favorite being "Valkyrie: The Women of
the Viking World."

00:04:45.199 --> 00:04:48.480
Gustavsson was also an accomplished rider
prior to the show, giving her an advantage

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:49.539
during the scenes on horseback.

00:04:49.539 --> 00:04:53.450
We can only hope she also did some strength
training, too, since Gustavsson's costume

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.360
is actually the heaviest of all the lead characters
on the show.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:00.560
There are more than 2,700 hand-cut leaves
that decorate her shieldmaiden armor.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.169
A big part of bringing the characters to life
on "Vikings" was the intricate costumes, designed

00:05:05.169 --> 00:05:09.090
in the show's final few seasons by costume
designer Susan O'Connor Cave.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:13.160
Cave is back for "Vikings: Valhalla," using
her keen eye for detail to enhance the story.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:16.419
For example, while the high-necked dresses
that Emma of Normandy wears on the show are

00:05:16.419 --> 00:05:20.360
indicative of her station and royal bearing,
they're also meant to act as her armor.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:24.020
They convey that although she doesn't charge
into battle like a shieldmaiden, she's still

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:25.710
a warrior in her own right.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:29.620
Similarly, although the devout Jarl Kåre
holds no official position in the church,

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:33.539
the silhouette of his costume is meant to
evoke a cassock, a garment traditionally worn

00:05:33.539 --> 00:05:35.240
by certain Christian clergy.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:39.259
Cave also incorporated the Vikings' cultural
and religious shifts into her costume designs

00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:40.620
for "Vikings: Valhalla."

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:44.580
You may notice richer colors and more chainmail
in the Viking’s costumes, meant to reflect

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:46.460
their transition to Christianity.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:50.210
Color and texture also plays a big role in
enhancing the characters, with the Greenlanders'

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:54.009
costumes meant to reflect their connection
to the earth, and the Vikings' costumes overall

00:05:54.009 --> 00:05:57.360
appearing more organic, favoring wools, linens,
and furs.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.930
The Saxons, meanwhile, will wear mostly velvets,
silks, and brocades with heavy embellishment,

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:04.500
highlighting the stark differences between
the cultures.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.789
While "Vikings" developed a reputation for
playing a bit fast and loose with history,

00:06:08.789 --> 00:06:11.259
the truth is a bit more complicated than that.

00:06:11.259 --> 00:06:15.509
Justin Pollard served as historical consultant
on both "Vikings" and "Vikings: Valhalla,"

00:06:15.509 --> 00:06:19.200
making him responsible for all the research
that helped inform the storylines.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:22.789
But researching Viking history isn't as simple
as just referencing old texts.

00:06:22.789 --> 00:06:24.819
As Jeb Stuart explained in his Netflix interview,

00:06:24.819 --> 00:06:28.330
"There are a million different ways to look
at history ... We don't know a whole lot about

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:31.970
the original Vikings — they didn't have
a written language, so the documentation is

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:32.970
tricky.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:36.319
They left us the sagas, but the sagas were
written 200 years after the end of this story,

00:06:36.319 --> 00:06:37.330
and they were written by Christians.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:41.000
We know archeologically what has been dug
up, but we're still discovering new things

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.139
about Viking culture."

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:45.509
Obviously, it's tricky to make an authentic
show about a group of people whose history

00:06:45.509 --> 00:06:49.440
was only written down centuries after the
fact, and even then, only by people outside

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:50.440
their culture.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:54.220
There's no simple "correct" version to defer
to, which means that many creative decisions

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:57.740
are based on historical interpretations rather
than historical facts.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:01.479
Still, the team behind "Vikings: Valhalla"
did their best to be as true to history as

00:07:01.479 --> 00:07:02.479
they could.

00:07:02.479 --> 00:07:03.479
Stuart says,

00:07:03.479 --> 00:07:04.800
"Ultimately, we've made an authentic show.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:08.389
Sometimes we've moved dates or characters
around a bit, but the historical pieces are

00:07:08.389 --> 00:07:09.389
all there."

00:07:09.389 --> 00:07:13.099
While things did begin to shift by the end
of the series, the original "Vikings" mostly

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:17.110
pitted the pagan Vikings against the English
Christians, with only isolated characters

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:19.979
like Athelstan expressing any interest in
bridging that gap.

00:07:19.979 --> 00:07:24.400
However, the series does feature the conversion
of the Viking warrior Rollo to Christianity,

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:27.199
at first in name only, but later on, in practice.

00:07:27.199 --> 00:07:31.069
A hundred years later, the religious landscape
in Europe looked very different, with many

00:07:31.069 --> 00:07:34.320
Vikings having followed in Rollo's footsteps
and converted to Christianity.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.650
"Vikings: Valhalla" will explore the conflict
between the pagan and Christian Vikings, with

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:41.960
both sides being well represented by the Viking
families in the series.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:45.250
Of its three main characters, Freydis and
Leif will both still worship Odin and the

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:47.430
old gods, while Harald is a Christian.

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:51.060
More broadly, while the Greenlander Vikings
will still be pagan, the Norwegian and Danish

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:53.680
Vikings will have largely converted to Christianity.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.360
According to Stuart, the two groups will clash
in "Vikings: Valhalla" in a religious civil

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:58.360
war.

00:07:58.360 --> 00:07:59.780
"Christians are my true enemy!"

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:01.400
"And you are mine!"

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:03.039
He said in the Netflix press kit,

00:08:03.039 --> 00:08:07.250
"It's a long and somewhat complex timeline,
but when our story is set, Christian Vikings

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:11.690
were mostly very sincere, very tough, and
very focused on absolute and total conversion

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:16.319
to Christianity — or violently wiping paganism
off the map entirely."

